		<div class="post" id="post-2127352">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2127352)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2127352">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1361261"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1412798376 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Oct 2014 19:59</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2127352">
									

<p>This is riddled with execution issues.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;line-by-line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide&nbsp;text</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Due to the nature of SCP-2699, each instance requires different containment protocols.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a filler sentence, as you then go on to lay out different containment protocols.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2699-1's front paws are to be checked every 24 hours for any wounds or infections, and if any are found they must be treated immediately to prevent further damage.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence strikes me as being kind of clunky. You could streamline it by saying &quot;SCP-2699-1 is to be checked every 24 hours and treated immediately in the case of wounds or infections in its front paws.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2699-1-a is to remain in archaeological storage unless required for analysis. Care is to be taken when removing this object from storage as it is very fragile.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't so much an execution problem particular to you as it is a sitewide practice that I wouldn't mind seeing cleaned up. But I don't know that it's necessary to give the skunk's poking rock its own subclassification. That seems almost humorously over-complex to me. Also, &quot;as it is very fragile&quot; sounds informal. &quot;Due to its fragility, object is to be handled with care&quot; would be an improvement, but it still doesn't really seem necessary to me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2699-2 must be contained in a padded cell. SCP-2699-2 is to be placed in a harness, allowing it to stand upright but not allowing it to move around its cell. To prevent muscle atrophy, at least 2 personnel are to take it out of its harness and move it around its cell twice a day for at least 5 minutes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is getting kind of overly complex. I know a lot of people say they skip over containment procedures entirely, so this may seem overly nitpicky, but if it's going to be here, it should be as concise and readable as possible. I'm not sure you need to go to the trouble of the specific exercise procedures (and I don't think that a total of 10 minutes of exercise a day is going to help anything), when you could probably just say that staff are required to assist it with physical activity or exercise on a daily basis or whatever.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and is to be fed cow, pig or horse meat.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Oddly specific. Couldn't you just say &quot;a diet of meat&quot; or something, or is there a reason it can't be fed goats, ocelots, gerbils, chickens, etc.?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>however they appear to be heavily mutated.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Someone with a more thorough background in biology may want to chime in here, but this is pinging my &quot;dubious use of a scientific term&quot; senses.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Another noticeable difference is that</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This lead-in to the sentence has a very &quot;school essay&quot; quality to it, and seems kind of odd considering the sentence before states that the most notable difference is already the differing stripe pattern. I think it might flow better if you just said something along the lines of &quot;SCP-2699-1 also lacks a tail&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(at first it was believed that the tail had been removed but further analysis confirms that a tail was never present)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like some sort of transition between &quot;removed&quot; and &quot;but&quot; is needed to prevent this from sounding like a run-on sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The anal scent glands of this specimen seem to give off a smell that is recognisable as lavender</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Seem to&quot;? It does or it doesn't. Especially given that the smell is apparently specifically recognizable.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>rather than the usual unpleasant smell that skunks are known for</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;That skunks are known for&quot; is unscientific tone. Also that's somewhat redundant combined with the &quot;usual&quot; descriptor.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When SCP-2699-1 was discovered, a stone tool (SCP-2699-1-a) was in its right paw which, after analysis, has been confirmed to look be similar in appearance to other Stone Age tools (specifically stone age axes).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The way the sentence is written implies that they tested the skunk's right paw and not the tool. I'm also pretty sure that they wouldn't be testing to see if it was &quot;similar in appearance&quot; to other Stone Age tools, but rather would testing it to see if it was similar in composition or artifice or something. Also, is &quot;Stone Age tool&quot; an accurate archeological descriptor?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2699-2 is a humanoid creature similar in appearance to a South-American male human</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;South-American&quot;, beyond being improperly hyphenated, is not anything close to a meaningful ethnic descriptor, considering you have an asston of different ethnicities in South America. I'm assuming that since he's an oh-so-hilarious Mexican stereotype, that he would be at the very least an Hispanic male human, or whatever.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It does, however, considerably increase the chance of SCP-2699-2 catching infections</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't think &quot;catching&quot; an infection is the preferred term in a scientific document.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2699-2 has no knee or elbow joints and, after X-ray analysis, it was revealed</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Inefficient use of language here. &quot;X-ray analysis has shown that SCP-2699-2 has no etc.&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>requiring help to stand upright again</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think &quot;assistance&quot; would sound more clinical than &quot;help&quot; here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2699-2's intelligence is considerably lower than that of an average human; it seems to have a basic understanding of its surroundings but is not capable of communication or solving simple logic puzzles such as opening doors.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not quite sure how they would make that determination if he's incapable of communication. Is he unintelligent, or is he brain-damaged or something? It seems like a distinction that researchers probably would have looked into.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2699-2 was originally found in a Mexican style poncho, large sombrero hat and a fake moustache (all of which are contained as SCP-2699-2-a) that had been stuck to its face and maracas which had been glued via unknown means to its hands.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For Christ's sake. Really?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2699-3 is a creature similar in appearance to a juvenile African elephant; however, a few differences have been noticed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The &quot;a few differences have been noticed&quot; is another example of the &quot;school essay&quot; thing you've got going here. I would just go into how it's different rather than announcing that it's different and here's why it's different.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2699-3's behaviour patterns are typical of a juvenile African elephant and can become increasingly stressed when in containment. It was decided that SCP-2699-3 would be allowed some minor freedom on Foundation-controlled grounds outside of containment to relieve its stress.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Stressed&quot; is a vague descriptor, to the point of meaninglessness. Was it depressed? Violent? Lethargic? Some combination thereof, or something else?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2699-4 is an unidentified species of reptile resembling Velociraptor mongoliensis as depicted in the popular movie &quot;Jurassic Park&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If it's just a normal velociraptor except bigger, wouldn't you just describe it that way? I know the Note at the End makes that comparison, but I don't see why scientists would.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Its behaviour is similar to that of a domestic house cat; It is very friendly to anyone who enters its containment cell</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Friendly&quot; is not a scientific term at all, and if my experience with cats holds any weight, I don't think they act in such a uniform manner from specimen to specimen that this is at all a meaningful description.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It has been discovered after a minor power cut in Building B5 of Site-131 that SCP-2699-4 becomes luminescent under low light conditions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What's a &quot;minor power cut&quot;? Seems to me the power either goes out or it doesn't. Also, unless they keep this thing in a lighted enclosure 24/7, wouldn't they have observed this earlier?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is unknown if the container itself is anomalous, as it is not known how it arrived at its discovery location (see addendum), and until further analysis can confirm its anomalous status it is to remain in secure storage.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This comes across as filler. I'm not really certain whether or not we need to know if the container that these things showed up in is anomalous or not.</p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I don't really like the core idea for this article much. It's implied that the dude writing the letter is an intern for either Abrahamic God or an Abrahamic God-like entity. So if that's the case, why is God sending counterfeit products to some goober on YouTube for review so that it can take &quot;legal action&quot;? Wouldn't it just God-magic some solution to its problem immediately? I dunno. Any possible sense behind this seems to have been ignored in the name of lolsogoofy; that works for enough of the readership for this to be positively rated, I guess, but I think that's kind of an overrepresented genre of skips in Series 3.</p>
<p>I'm not a huge fan of the racist Mexican caricature either. Like, I get that it's the counterfeiter in the story doing it, but it doesn't even really make internal sense with the rest of the anomaly. The counterfeiter doesn't understand enough about animals to know that skunks aren't sapient tool-users or that elephants aren't amphibians, but it's savvy enough about human culture to portray a Mexican dude in a stereotypical manner? I get that racism exists in the world and that it is sometimes necessary to tell a story, but it's one of those things that you'd better have a good reason for and you'd better do well, and neither condition is really met here.</p>
<p>Also, you describe the animals as being sapient, but you don't really go anywhere with it. Especially with the frogaphant. You mention that it's &quot;stressed&quot;. That could actually be kind of interesting. What does a frog-elephant abomination think, anyway? But you do absolutely nothing with that element of the story after introducing it, which renders it clutter in the context of the piece.</p>
<p>Really, though, the conceptual issues ultimately take a back seat to the execution. For the reasons outlined in the line-by-line, I don't really view the writing here as up to mainlist standards.</p>

